The collection arrangement is as follows:</p><p>
Series 1. Peace and Social Justice Program, Vice President, Bradford K. Smith subject files, 1996-2005.</p>  </arrangement>
  <scopecontent id="796fa9502399bc32b7ca9ecde38f9018">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>This collection holds the office files of Ford Foundation executive staff member Maya Harris. However, the records in the collection were not actually created by Harris. They all originate from the Office of Ford Foundation Peace and Social Justice Program Vice President Bradford K. Smith. Inventory information provided by Ford to the RAC described the materials as historical documents, suggesting that Harris had inherited Smith's files and that her Office absorbed those documents into its own records. Harris served as Vice President for Ford's Democracy, Rights, and Justice Program between 2008 and 2013. This program likely succeeded the Peace and Social Justice Program that Smith served. Future accessions may expand the collection so that it includes documents produced by Harris' Office.</p><p>
Collection materials chiefly consist of correspondence, agendas, notes, grant recommendations, reports, and speech transcripts. They originate from between 1996 and 2005, all within the timeframe of Smith's tenure as Peace and Social Justice Program Vice President.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <bioghist id="47bb8ae14fd997a0aa08ae42d8d2eac8">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Maya Harris served as Vice President of the Ford Foundation's Democracy, Rights and Justice Program between 2008 and 2013. In this position, she was responsible for grantmaking decisions regarding the promotion of greater democratic participation and the protection for human rights.</p><p>After leaving Ford in 2013, she continued pursuing her interests in politics and philanthropy. She worked as a Senior Advisor for Hillary Clinton's 2016 Presidential Campaign and joined the Arkus Foundation's Board of Directors in 2017. She is the sister of U.S. Vice President Kamala Harris.
  </p>  </bioghist>
